              {
                "text": "I need to identify the box color, forming rectangular shapes with min dimension over 2, and find the noise color as the least common color besides the box color. The output should be the largest box subgrid with the box color swapped for the noise color, and I'm working out how to construct three example inputs that satisfy this pattern reliably, including edge cases around background color counts.\n\nFor my first design, I'm laying out a 12x12 grid with two boxes in color 3 (a small 4x4 and a large 6x6) plus scattered noise dots in color 2 placed both inside and outside the boxes, making sure the noise pixels stay as separate single-cell objects rather than merging with the box outlines.\n\nFor the second pair, I'm switching to a 10x13 grid using box color 4 and noise color 6, with a 3x3 box and a 5x5 box, again scattering isolated noise cells around both shapes so the larger box ends up containing more noise pixels than the smaller one.\n\nNow I'm moving to pair 3, which uses three separate boxes with box color 1 and noise color 7 on a 14x14 grid.\n\nFilling in the remaining rows, I complete the grid with scattered 7s inside the box interior and confirm the objects are found using non-diagonal adjacency.\n\n",
